<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lathe">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lathe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A lathe is a machine tool that turns a workpiece about an axis while tools cut, sand, drill or shape it into a form symmetrical about that axis. Clear evidence of turning comes from the 6th century BCE: fragments of a wooden bowl in an Etruscan tomb in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and two wooden dishes with turned rims from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. A painting in the tomb of Petosiris in 4th-century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is the first known picture of a lathe, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> rotary lathes sharpened tools and weapons by about 400 BCE. Andrey Nartov built a lathe with a tool-carrying carriage and gears in 1718, and by the 1770s precision lathes with a slide-rest had become practical.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lathe.jpg" alt="Lathe" width="330" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glenn McKechnie · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HwacheonCentreLathe_460x1000.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The lathe is an ancient tool. There is tenuous evidence for a turned artefact at a Mycenaean Greek site as early as the 13th or 14th century BCE, but clear evidence begins in the 6th century BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, rotary lathes were used about 400 BCE to sharpen tools and weapons on an industrial scale. Pliny describes the use of a lathe for turning soft stone in his Natural History.</p><p>Precision metal-cutting lathes developed organically in the lead-up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, with many inventors contributing, and they were critical to the manufacture of the mechanical inventions of that period. The literature makes many claims for the person who invented the precision lathe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The most common design is the universal or parallel lathe, built on a bed, usually a horizontal beam, with a headstock holding the spindle and a tailstock that can be moved to suit the length of the work. A workpiece held between the two centres is supported at both ends and can take more force from the tool. Work held only at the headstock, on a faceplate or in a chuck, is called face work and must be cut more gently. Re-mounting the piece on a new axis allows eccentric turning, a technique used for camshafts and some chair legs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Woodworking lathes are the oldest variety apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potters-wheel">potter's wheel</a>, and all others descend from them. The turner shapes the wood with hand-held tools resting on a tool-rest and then smooths it with sandpaper. In a metalworking lathe a hardened cutting tool fixed to a tool-post or turret is moved against the work by lead-screws, and the same machines cut threads. Thomas Blanchard's copying lathe, which reproduced a pattern, revolutionised gun stock making in the 1820s, and Andrey Nartov designed a copying lathe for ornamental turning, now in the Hermitage Museum, for making medals and guilloche patterns.</p><p>Other kinds serve other trades. Watchmakers' lathes turn parts such as balance staffs, and the original watchmaker's turns was a simple dead-centre lathe whose workpiece was rotated by a bow wrapped around it. Glass-working lathes turn a hollow vessel over a flame, and metal-spinning lathes press sheet metal over a mandrel. Immanuel Nobel invented the rotary lathe that peels logs into veneer, and recording lathes cut the grooves of wax cylinders and discs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lathe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2007 researchers at Stanford University reported that the Chinese purple pigment drew on knowledge gained by Taoist alchemists trying to make jade, and microscope marks give evidence of the earliest industrial use of <b>lathes</b> for polishing.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
